Google can only crawl what it can discover. In the thread, the guidance is that if your PHP dynamic pages are not linked from other pages, Google may not find them even if you have a sitemap. Google may choose to use the sitemap or may prefer following links, but if the pages aren’t linked, discovery is unreliable.

So the closest supported advice is:
- make the dynamic pages reachable through normal site links, preferably in a human-focused navigation structure;
- use a sitemap as a help, not as the only discovery method;
- if the pages are valuable to users, link them and let Google recrawl them over time to notice updates.

Use glob() to list the files in the user’s directory, then loop through the results and display them in a table. For example:

```php
<?php
foreach (glob("*.*") as $filename) {
  if (is_file($filename)) {
    echo $filename . " size " . filesize($filename) . "\n";
  }
}
?>
```

This covers how to display files from a folder in PHP. If each user has their own folder, point glob() at that user’s directory path instead of "*.*".

A reasonable approach is to use webserver basic authentication to prevent unauthorized reading, and MediaWiki permissions to control writing.

For write lockdown, the suggested MediaWiki settings disable anonymous create/edit actions and allow those actions for sysop only:
$wgGroupPermissions['*']['createaccount'] = false;
$wgGroupPermissions['*']['edit'] = false;
$wgGroupPermissions['*']['editpage'] = false;
$wgGroupPermissions['*']['edittalk'] = false;
$wgGroupPermissions['*']['createpage'] = false;
$wgGroupPermissions['*']['createtalk'] = false;

$wgGroupPermissions['sysop']['createaccount'] = true;
$wgGroupPermissions['sysop']['edit'] = true;
$wgGroupPermissions['sysop']['editpage'] = true;
$wgGroupPermissions['sysop']['edittalk'] = true;
$wgGroupPermissions['sysop']['createpage'] = true;
$wgGroupPermissions['sysop']['createtalk'] = true;

If you expect many users or changing privilege tiers over time, the best-supported advice here is to use a plugin or customize MediaWiki for exactly that access model.

You’re almost there. The issue is that `#menu_bar` and `#content_area` are block elements, so they naturally stack vertically.

To place `#content_area` next to `#menu_bar`, set them both to float:

```css
#menu_bar {
    float: left;
    width: 20%;
}

#content_area {
    float: left;
    width: 75%;
}
```

Make sure their combined widths fit inside the container, including margins and borders. If needed, clear the float after them so the footer stays below.

Yes. `<h1>` to `<h6>` are different heading levels, so they are not equivalent for structure. In the provided answer, the focus is that `hgroup` is not an official HTML tag and therefore is discouraged and has almost certainly no SEO value.

For SEO, the closest guidance supported here is:
- use proper heading tags for document structure;
- don’t rely on `hgroup` for SEO;
- any SEO difference from using one heading level over another is not addressed here, beyond the fact that heading tags should be used semantically.

If you mean repeating the same long title on many links, the supported approach here is to use JavaScript to assign that title after the page loads. Put a shared class on each link, then loop through them and set the title once in script.

Example:

<a class="t" href="goThere1">My 1st link</a>

<script>
var titleString = "A very long string common to all my links";
var links = document.getElementsByClassName('t');
for (var i = 0; i < links.length; i++) {
  links[i].setAttribute('title', titleString);
}
</script>

This reduces HTML size, but the titles may not be seen by search engines or some screen readers.

If you need to change the URL dynamically, do it in PHP before any output. Check the request path with `$_SERVER["REQUEST_URI"]`, and if it matches something like `/index.php`, send a redirect:

```php
if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_URI"] == "/index.php") {
    header("Location: http://myapp.com/");
    exit;
}
```

That’s the cleaner approach described. JavaScript can also change the URL, but the PHP redirect is preferred. If you’re on shared hosting where `.htaccess` can’t be edited, this PHP method is the supported workaround.

